      "question": {
        ":question/id": "be240e42-6dba-510d-b4fb-08e71ec9aed0",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Given $f(x)=e^{x}$ and $h(x)=- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that $f(x)=e^{x}$ and $h(x)=- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(e^{x})^{\\prime}\\cdot (- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(e^{x})\\cdot (- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(e^{x})\\cdot (- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(e^{x})\\cdot (- 4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})}{16\\sin^2 x}\\\\ &=\\frac{4e^x(\\cos x-\\sin x)}{16\\sin^2 x}\\\\ &=\\frac{2e^x(\\cos x-\\sin x)}{8\\sin^2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "a861bb65-8377-5fbd-814a-6205617d2893",
